  "content": {
    "title": "UConn men's basketball falls to Michigan in NCAA title game",
    "text": "INDIANAPOLIS — Can't win 'em all, though the UConn men's basketball team usually does in national championship games.Not this time. Not quite.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢UConn's Braylon Mullins, left, and Jayden Ross reflect on the team's loss to Michigan in the NCAA National Championship Game at Lucas Oil Stadium in Indianapolis, Ind., Monday, April 6, 2026.Dave Zajac/Hearst Connecticut MediaFor the first time in seven trips to the national title tilt, the Huskies won't bring a trophy back to the Basketball Capital of the World.Article continues below this adThe confetti instead fell on Michigan, which concluded a dominant run through the NCAA Tournament with a 69-63 win over UConn on Monday night at Lucas Oil Stadium.\"Your heart breaks,\" Dan Hurley said. \"But, that's the risk you've got to be willing to take. You've got to be willing to commit everything you have to your purpose or your passion or what you do. When you have the highest of goals, sometimes you're going to have your heart broken.\"Make CT Insider a preferred source on Google to see more of our journalism when you search.Add Preferred SourceThe Wolverines took advantage of a huge edge in size, length and trips to the free throw line to win the program's second national title. Michigan becomes the first Big Ten team to win the men's national title since Tom Izzo and Michigan State in 2000. The Wolverines finish 37-3, the same record the 2024 UConn squad finished with while rolling to its second straight NCAA title.Article continues below this adStill, Michigan looked a far cry from that dominant Husky team of two years ago for most of the contest. The Wolverines misfired on their first 10 3-point attempts and couldn't get anything going in transition for most of the night. Elliot Cadeau finally broke the seal just over seven minutes into the second half with Michigan's first 3-pointer of the game, giving it its biggest lead (48-37) at the time and causing Hurley to call timeout.UConn head coach Dan Hurley, left, reacts during the second half of the NCAA college basketball tournament national championship game against Michigan at the Final Four, Monday, April 6, 2026, in Indianapolis. (AP Photo/Abbie Parr)Abbie Parr/APThe Huskies, as they do, kept fighting, getting to within seven with a chance to close to within four when Braylon Mullins lined up a 3-pointer with just under 11 minutes left. He misfired.Article continues below this adWith 2 1/2 minutes left in the game (and in his record-breaking career), Alex Karaban knocked down a 3-pointer to get UConn to within six. Tarris Reed Jr. then came up with a steal near midcourt, and Solo Ball had an off-balance layup attempt to get the Huskies to within four.He couldn't convert. Trey McKenney countered with Michigan's second 3-pointer of the night.The Huskies weren't done. Ball banked in a 3-pointer with 37.2 seconds left, and Michigan's Roddy Gayle Jr. missed consecutive free throws (just the third missed freebies of the night for the Wolverines). Karaban (who else?) had a chance to make it a one-point game with a long 3-point attempt, but he front-rimmed it.\"I just wanted to shoot it quickly, give us more time to press,\" Karaban explained. \"It was short, obviously, just because it was further back.\"Article continues below this adMcKenney hit a pair of free throws to seal the deal for Michigan.INDIANAPOLIS, INDIANA - APRIL 06: Yaxel Lendeborg #23 of the Michigan Wolverines celebrates in front of Alex Karaban #11 of the UConn Huskies in the National Championship of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Lucas Oil Stadium on April 06, 2026 in Indianapolis, Indiana. (Photo by Jamie Schwaberow/NCAA Photos via Getty Images)Jamie Schwaberow/NCAA Photos via Getty Images\"I'll take how we went out,\" Hurley said. \"It was a soldier's death. We all went out on our shields.\"UConn finishes 34-6. The Huskies are now 18-4 in March Madness games under Hurley, who is now 20-6 overall. Karaban finishes his UConn career at 18-2 in NCAA tourney games.Article continues below this adMichigan led 33-29 at halftime despite missing all eight of its 3-point attempts. The Wolverines used a steady parade to the foul line (11-for-12) that clearly peeved Hurley. Karaban and Ball each had eight points at halftime, though Ball, Reed and Silas Demary Jr. each picked up a pair of fouls and Karaban was called for a hook-and-hold flagrant.Axel Lendeborg, Michigan's leading scorer and All-American forward, was clearly compromised by a knee injury suffered two nights earlier in the national semifinals. He had just four points at halftime on 1-for-5 shooting, but Morez Johnson Jr. (10 points) and Aday Mara (six) picked up the pace down low, while Cadeau added seven.INDIANAPOLIS, INDIANA - APRIL 06: Alex Karaban #11 of the UConn Huskies reacts during the first half against the Michigan Wolverines in the National Championship of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Lucas Oil Stadium on April 06, 2026 in Indianapolis, Indiana. (Photo by Jamie Schwaberow/NCAA Photos via Getty Images)Jamie Schwaberow/NCAA Photos via Getty ImagesEven with the 6-foot-9 Lendeborg struggling, Michigan's size advantage was glaring. The Wolverines blocked six shots, altered at least a half-dozen more, and constantly had their hands in passing lanes and making life tough for UConn ballhandlers.Article continues below this adDespite all that, the Huskies outrebounded Michigan 46-39, including 22 on the offensive glass. \"It speaks to how these guys ripped their hearts out of their chests and left it on the floor,\" Hurley noted. Michigan hit just 2 of 15 3-point attempts for the game but made up for it at the foul line, converting 25 of 28. UConn was whistled for 22 fouls, Michigan just 13.Hurley ranted at the refs for much of the game, but had a more measured response to the foul discrepancy afterwards.Article continues below this ad\"It's part of it,\" he reasoned. \"Sometimes you get a good whistle, sometimes you don't. I thought we were well-positioned in the first half to go into the locker room with a lead. I thought going down four, Solo was the one guy who was shooting well, along with AK. And obviously not having Tarris in there to deal with those guys was tough.\"UConn's Jayden Ross speaks to members of the media after the Huskies lost to Michigan in the NCAA National Championship Game at Lucas Oil Stadium in Indianapolis, Ind., Monday, April 6, 2026.Dave Zajac/Hearst Connecticut MediaUltimately, the Huskies didn't shoot well enough, either: just 31 percent from the floor and 9-for-33 (27 percent) from 3. Karaban led UConn with 17 points but was 3-for-10 from 3, while Reed had another double-double (13 and 14) but missed eight of 12 shots from the floor, including a 3. Ball, who said he took two medical shots just to get on the floor due to a foot injury that kept him out of practice on Sunday, finished with 11. Mullins was just 4-for-17 (3-for-10 from 3) and also added 11.Demary, compromised by a high ankle sprain for the past few weeks, finished with just two points before fouling out.Article continues below this ad\"If we made some more 3's, we'd be on the other side of it,\" Karaban reasoned. \"But in the end, we didn't go out like suckers. We fought, we made it a battle, and we have a lot to be proud of. Time's going to heal everything.\"That Huskies fell short of both Big East regular-season and tournament championships this season.\"We were able to validate our season by getting to the championship game and really making Michigan have to fight and earn it tonight,\" Hurley said. \"There's no moral victory, but I thought this team gave everything it had and got us as far as it could get.\"UConn was dead in the water against East Region top seed Duke eight days earlier, trailing by 19 late in the first half and never getting over the hump until Mullins' miracle 35-footer with :04 left sent the Huskies to their eighth Final Four.Article continues below this adINDIANAPOLIS, INDIANA - APRIL 06: The UConn Huskies stand on the court before playing against the Michigan Wolverines in the National Championship of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Lucas Oil Stadium on April 06, 2026 in Indianapolis, Indiana. (Photo by Jamie Schwaberow/NCAA Photos via Getty Images)Jamie Schwaberow/NCAA Photos via Getty ImagesUConn dusted off Illinois in Saturday night's national semifinals. But a fourth straight win over a Big Ten team in the tournament, not to mention a third national title in four years, fell a few shots short.\"We didn't come here for second place,\" Hurley noted. \"I'll accept my (runner-up) watch. It's a nice watch, too. I don't want to diminish the value of the watch. But, we came here to hang a banner and win a ring.\"Article continues below this adStill, Hurley believes this team will occupy a special place in Husky lore, despite just missing out on the ultimate prize.\"It's going to be one our fans are going to remember for a long time because of the ride that they gave them, at this time of year, especially. We're going to be in this position a lot.\"Score exclusive insights and analysis on UConn athletics.April 6, 2026|Updated April 7, 2026 2:19 a.m.Dave Borges is a sports reporter for Hearst Connecticut Media Group covering the UConn men's basketball team. An award-winning journalist, Dave has covered the Huskies since 2007, including coverage of five Final Fours and four national championships (2011, 2014, 2023 and 2024). He has been an Associated Press Top 25 poll voter since 2009 and was named NSMA Connecticut Sportswriter of the Year in 2019. Dave has authored three books: \"Hurley's Heroes: UConn's Return to College Basketball's Elite,\" \"Rebound: The Incredible Story of UConn Basketball's Comeback from Defeat to Dominance,\" and \"Images of Baseball: The Pawtucket Red Sox.\" He also covers baseball and was a beat writer chronicling the 2004 World Series-champion Boston Red Sox. Dave has been a Baseball Hall of Fame voter since 2014. He can be reached at david.borges@hearstmediact.com.",
